  note     = {kind=finding by=agda-oracle axis=a. THE Ⓑ15 PRECONDITION DISSOLVES ON READING THE TOOL. scratch/rename_at_source.py:362 parses --site= straight from argv as a string. :172 sets targets to [site] when site is given, else the tree-wide glob. :146 classify_file returns "renamed" on (f == home_file or site) BEFORE any ambiguity test. Its own selftest at :343 asserts it -- "...and --site still forces the rename, so the escape hatch survives". Nothing reads a build log. The compiler error was only ever the DISCOVERY channel telling an operator WHICH file, and check_clause_audit already supplies that as (module, line, target, name) -- 166 rows in 7.45s CPU measured on the working tree. ⚑ BUT --site ALSO BYPASSES THE AMBIGUITY GUARD, which is the real reason an arbitrated site mattered. --site is an ASSERTION that this file is safe, not a check that it is. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): rename_at_source --site=FILE is a PURE STRING SELECTOR and consumes no build output. Measured by reading scratch/rename_at_source.py: line 362 parses --site= from argv; line 172 targets=[site] if site else glob(...); line 146 classify_file returns 'renamed' immediately if f==home_file or site, BEFORE the ambiguity checks. The selftest at :343 asserts exactly this ('--site still forces the rename, so the escape hatch survives'). So the Ⓑ15 precondition 'there are no fresh arbitrating errors right now' does NOT block --site arbitration: the census row (module,line,target,name) from check_clause_audit already names the site. The build error was only ever the DISCOVERY channel for which file, never an INPUT to the tool.}
