  note     = {kind=finding by=agda-oracle. B14. The brief stated ZERO index blind spots in the 166. FALSE. --provides Substrate.Cocycles.V4Signature.V4GroupSetoid returns 0 name(s) and --reexports returns 0 sites, which reads as a severed barrel. But Read of agda/Substrate/Cocycles/V4Signature/V4GroupSetoid.agda shows the whole body is one line, V4-Group-Setoid = to-setoid V4-Group, a binding with NO type signature. def_index's parser _def_index_uncached matches data/record heads, pattern, constructor, module, and 'name :' signatures -- never a bare = binding. So the module exports the name and the 5 rows naming it are method artifacts. _clause_scan already models ONE such blind spot (a target that re-exports gets verdict UNKNOWN via the opaque set). The signature-less binding is a SECOND, unmodelled one. Provenance of 166 -- scripts/check_clause_audit.py --progress, working tree, 15.4s, 6345 clause names over 3598 modules. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): kind=finding by=agda-oracle. Ⓑ14 THE 166 NONEXPORT ROWS ARE NOT ALL LATENT BUILD FAILURES; the brief's 'ZERO index blind spots' is false. clause_audit's NONEXPORT verdict rests on def_index, whose parser matches only 'name :' SIGNATURES. A signature-less binding is invisible to it, so a module that genuinely exports the name is reported as not exporting it. WITNESS: 5 of the 166 rows name Substrate.Cocycles.V4Signature.V4GroupSetoid / V4-Group-Setoid; --provides that module returns '0 name(s)', but READING agda/Substrate/Cocycles/V4Signature/V4GroupSetoid.agda shows its entire body is 'V4-Group-Setoid = to-setoid V4-Group' -- a binding with no type signature. The module DOES export the name. Those 5 rows are false positives. The disambiguator was READING THE FILE, never the count, exactly as the dispatch brief required.}
}

@misc{agda-B14-head-blob-is-the-barrel-resolver,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A severed barrel's name-to-leaf map is recoverable with no build and no surviving re-export by reading the barrel's HEAD blob (agda_defs --at HEAD <Mod> --diff), which prints the deleted public line that carried the names -- and it resolves cases all three working-tree tiers report as ambiguous or unresolved.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=agda-oracle. B14. This is why scope_fix --seed cannot reach these barrels and what CAN. --seed reads WORKING-TREE public re-exports and the severed barrels have none (--reexports Substrate.Groups.Coxeter.Cyclic.Existential -- 0 site(s) in 0 of 3598 module(s)). WITNESS -- Groups.Capabilities.xFreeCyclic.Witness asks Cyclic.Existential for normalize and normalize-canonical. Working-tree tier 1, --provides Existential, 6 names, neither present. Tier 2, --under Existential, 1 module. Tier 3, --imports Existential, 13 modules, none provides normalize. Tree-wide agda_defs normalize returns 9 homes, AMBIGUOUS, so a name-match rebind would mis-target. But --at HEAD Existential --diff shows the DELETED line open import Substrate.Groups.Coxeter.ListPresentation Gen Canonical-ex c-eps insert insert-canonical-ex public, and --provides ListPresentation returns WithLemmas, normalize, normalize-canonical, normalize-distrib. Same shape at Groups.SemidirectProduct, where the diff shows open import Substrate.Groups.V4-Embedding using (v-of-axis ...) LOST its public keyword, which is exactly the 4 SemidirectProduct-to-V4-Embedding rows. So the resolution order for a barrel-map mode should be HEAD-blob re-export FIRST, then subtree leaves, then barrel imports, then tree-wide with refusal on multi-home. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): kind=finding by=agda-oracle. B14. The severed barrels' name->leaf map is recoverable WITHOUT a build and WITHOUT any surviving re-export, by reading the barrel's HEAD blob via agda_defs.at_revision. --at HEAD <Mod> --diff prints the deleted 'public' line that carried the names. WITNESS -- Groups.Capabilities.xFreeCyclic.Witness asks Coxeter.Cyclic.Existential for normalize and normalize-canonical. All three working-tree tiers fail: --provides Existential lists 6 names, neither of them. --under Existential is 1 module. --imports Existential is 13 modules, none providing normalize. Tree-wide, agda_defs normalize returns 9 homes -- AMBIGUOUS, must refuse. But --at HEAD Existential --diff shows a DELETED line, open import Substrate.Groups.Coxeter.ListPresentation Gen Canonical-ex c-eps insert insert-canonical-ex public, and --provides ListPresentation returns exactly WithLemmas, normalize, normalize-canonical, normalize-distrib. The HEAD blob resolves what the working tree cannot, and it dissolves the ambiguity that would otherwise force a refusal.}
}

@misc{agda-B16-file-reads-need-a-vfs-layer,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {every structural reader opens paths directly, so none can answer a question about a prior revision without a caller staging text to a temp file first},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=main. Operator item 2026-08-25. Route file reads through a KIO-shaped VFS so git-at-a-rev and the working tree are the same call. Forcing case: agda_defs.provides reads disk, so scope_fix.seed_map hand-rolls its own git show. Same shape in pycodemod, mdstruct, bibstruct. Research owed on whether a python module already provides the pattern. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓑ BACKLOG, ARCHITECTURAL, operator 2026-08-25: anything that reads from a file should route through a VFS layer so it can be pointed at git instead of the working tree. KIO-shaped — URI-addressed with pluggable backends, so file and git-at-a-rev are the same call. ⚑ THE FORCING CASE, MEASURED THIS SESSION: agda_defs.provides reads files from disk, so it cannot be reused on HEAD text directly. That is why scope_fix.seed_map hand-rolls its own git show HEAD path instead of calling the structural reader, and why a barrel-to-leaf map needs a bespoke composition rather than one query against two revisions. The tree already wants the capability and pays for its absence three ways: seed_map re-implements git access, agda_defs --at HEAD exists as a SEPARATE mode rather than a parameter, and any comparison across revisions becomes a manual composition. ⚑⚑ AND THE SAME SHAPE RECURS OUTSIDE AGDA. pycodemod, mdstruct, bibstruct all open paths directly, so none can answer a question about a prior revision without a caller staging text to a temp file first. A reader parameterized on its source answers what changed between two revisions as ONE query. ⚑ UNWITNESSED because no command distinguishes true from false here — it is a design item, not a defect with a discriminator, and the honest kind for that is 2 rather than a standing green. RESEARCH OWED: whether a python module already provides the KIO pattern, since building a fourth file-access spelling would be the defect this repo keeps paying for.}
}

@misc{agda-B14-unknown-flag-falls-through-to-name-lookup,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {agda_defs.py has no unknown-flag refusal -- an unrecognised mode falls through its if-chain to the bare-name branch and prints a plausible 'no definer' answer at exit 0, indistinguishable from a real resolution miss.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=agda-oracle. B14. agda_defs' CLI is a chain of 'if --mode in sys.argv' branches terminating in 'if _args:' at :5709, which treats every remaining operand as a NAME. WITNESS, measured before any edit -- python3 scratch/agda_defs.py --barrel-map Substrate.Groups.Coxeter.Cyclic.Base printed the module name then '(no definer in agda/Substrate -- may be Agda.Primitive/stdlib)' and exited 0. A reader probing for a mode that does not exist gets a confident negative about the TREE instead of a refusal about the QUERY. Same shape as agda-F13, where pycodemod --literal swallowed a flag-shaped operand, except pycodemod REFUSES and agda_defs does not. The asymmetry is visible inside agda_defs itself -- --provides <unknown-module> exits 2 saying 'a fact about the QUERY, not about the tree', so the refusal discipline is present for a mode's OPERAND and absent for the mode NAME. This matters for a barrel-map dispatch specifically -- every probe for the not-yet-built mode returns a false negative that reads as an answer. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): kind=finding by=agda-oracle. B14. agda_defs.py has NO unknown-flag refusal. Its CLI is a chain of 'if "--mode" in sys.argv' branches ending in 'if _args:' which treats every remaining operand as a NAME to resolve. A misspelled or not-yet-built flag therefore falls through and prints a PLAUSIBLE answer for a question nobody asked. WITNESS -- before building anything, python3 scratch/agda_defs.py --barrel-map Substrate.Groups.Coxeter.Cyclic.Base printed 'Substrate.Groups.Coxeter.Cyclic.Base' followed by '(no definer in agda/Substrate -- may be Agda.Primitive/stdlib)' and exited 0. That output is indistinguishable from a real name-resolution miss. This is the agda-F13 shape (pycodemod --literal swallowing a flag-shaped operand) in a second tool, and pycodemod REFUSES where agda_defs does not. Contrast agda_defs --provides <unknown>, which correctly exits 2 with 'NOT A MODULE IN THE INDEX ... a fact about the QUERY, not about the tree'. The refusal exists for one mode's operand and not for the mode name itself.}
}

@misc{agda-B17-chokepoint-modules-do-not-import-word,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The three modules B15 names as masked by the Coxeter.Word _++_ ClashingDefinition have no import path to Coxeter.Word, so the chokepoint reading is unsupported and the 166 NONEXPORT rows are not masked by it at all.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=agda-oracle unification=asserted axis=b. B17. agda_defs --between returns 0 edges from each of Groups.Subgroup, Lojban.Fragment.Example1MiNelciDo and Cardinality.Product to Substrate.Groups.Coxeter.Word. Positive control Strict2Monoid to Word returns 2 edges, so --between is not silently empty. check_clause_audit.py --quiet is static, launches no agda, and printed all 166 NONEXPORT rows with no _++_ row present. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓑ17 THE MASKING FINDING agda-B15-single-chokepoint-masks-all NAMES THREE MODULES THAT HAVE NO IMPORT PATH TO THE CLASH SITE. B15 records that Groups/Subgroup.agda, Lojban/Fragment/Example1MiNelciDo.agda and Cardinality/Product.agda each aborted at Coxeter/Word.agda:38 ClashingDefinition. Re-derived 2026-08-26 with agda_defs --between: all three return '0 edge(s), 0 source module(s), 0 target module(s)' to Substrate.Groups.Coxeter.Word. POSITIVE CONTROL: agda_defs --between Substrate.Groups.Capabilities.Strict2Monoid Substrate.Groups.Coxeter.Word returns 2 edge(s), so --between is not silently empty. Groups.Subgroup --depends lists 12 packages / 26 modules with no Coxeter at all. So EITHER the probe compiled a wider unit than the named module (a whole-tree or package-level agda invocation whose first error is simply the tree's first error, in which case 'masks' is an artifact of compiling everything, not a chokepoint the three modules share), OR the error text was mis-attributed to the three modules. Both readings destroy the load-bearing part of B15: the claim that ONE clash gates THESE modules' NotInScope rows. What survives is only that agda halts at the first scope error within a single compilation unit. ⚑ THE 166 NONEXPORT ROWS ARE NOT MASKED AT ALL: check_clause_audit.py --quiet is a STATIC reader that launches no compiler and printed all 166 rows in full on 2026-08-26, with no _++_ row among them.}
}

@misc{agda-B17-list-is-already-minimal,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Foundation.List is an already-minimal 8-name leaf, so no decomposition can remove _++_ from it and the Coxeter.Word clash is intrinsic, making rename-at-source the correct repair with the decompose-first rule satisfied vacuously rather than skipped.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=agda-oracle unification=entailed axis=ab. B17, a second instance of agda-F8-decomposition-does-not-dissolve. agda_defs --provides Foundation.List gives 8 names and --imports gives 1 edge to Foundation.Level. Word.agda line 19 bare-imports Foundation.List and line 38 defines its own top-level _++_. agda_defs --importers Coxeter.Word reports 182 external importers, and rename_at_source self-documents a 1-in-10 importer false-positive rate, so the rename needs a build this dispatch was forbidden to run. Cited points: agda-F8-decomposition-does-not-dissolve measured Foundation.Nat at 3 names still colliding, and this measures Foundation.List at 8 names with the same outcome. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓑ17 DECOMPOSITION CANNOT DISSOLVE THE _++_ CLASH, AND THIS IS A SECOND INSTANCE OF agda-F8-decomposition-does-not-dissolve. The ban-decompose playbook's ordering rule says decompose the provider first because most collisions evaporate. Measured 2026-08-26: agda_defs --provides Substrate.Foundation.List returns EIGHT names (List, [], _::_, _++_, ++, ::, foldl, foldr). It is already a leaf -- agda_defs --imports Substrate.Foundation.List shows ONE import (Foundation.Level). There is no split of an 8-name leaf that removes _++_ from it, because _++_ is one of the eight reasons the module exists. The clash is intrinsic: agda/Substrate/Groups/Coxeter/Word.agda bare-imports Foundation.List at line 19 and defines its own top-level _++_ at line 38, in a flat namespace. Decomposing Foundation.Vec is likewise irrelevant, since agda_defs --between Foundation.Vec Foundation.List returns 0 edges and neither imports the other. SO THE HONEST ORDERING VERDICT IS: rename-at-source is the correct repair here and the decompose-first rule is satisfied vacuously, NOT skipped. ⚑ BUT THE RENAME IS NOT SMALL: agda_defs --importers Substrate.Groups.Coxeter.Word reports 190 edges in and 182 EXTERNAL importers, and rename_at_source carries a self-documented 1-in-10 false-positive rate across importers, so roughly 18 expected bad edits that only a full build could find. Word.agda's own header says it exists to 'avoid Data.List clashing-definition friction' -- the module created to dodge a clash now causes one.}
}

@misc{agda-B17-no-shared-scope-census,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {No mode intersects the provider set with the consumer set, so a --collide provider census over-reports live clashes because it is blind to using-filtered imports and to the nesting depth of a definition.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=agda-oracle unification=asserted axis=c. B17, WORK not a wall. agda_defs --collide _++_ named 5 providers. Reading the files refuted two of them: S5TwoFuelFix imports Foundation.List with a using filter that omits _++_ and defines its own inside module TwoFuel, and Strict2Monoid's _++_ is a record field inside record Strict2MonoidCapability. toolmodes --find co-import returns 0 hits over 603 declared modes. Unwitnessed because no command distinguishes an absent mode from a mode under an untried spelling. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓑ17 NO MODE ANSWERS 'WHICH MODULES BARE-IMPORT TWO PROVIDERS OF THE SAME NAME', WHICH IS THE ONLY QUESTION THAT DECIDES A CLASH. agda_defs --collide <name> gives the PROVIDER set. agda_defs --importers <Mod> gives the CONSUMER set. Nothing intersects them, so establishing whether a collision is LIVE requires reading each candidate file by hand. Measured cost on 2026-08-26: --collide _++_ named 5 providers, and deciding which pairs actually share a scope took 4 --imports calls plus 2 whole-file Reads. THE READ WAS DECISIVE AND THE INDEX WAS NOT: agda_defs reported Substrate.S5.S5TwoFuelFix and Substrate.Groups.Capabilities.Strict2Monoid as _++_ providers alongside Foundation.List, but reading the files shows S5TwoFuelFix imports Foundation.List with using (List, [], _::_) and defines its _++_ inside module TwoFuel, while Strict2Monoid's _++_ is a record FIELD inside record Strict2MonoidCapability. NEITHER IS A CLASH. So a provider census over-reports live clashes because it is blind to (a) using-filtered imports and (b) nesting depth of the definition. toolmodes --find co-import returns 0 hits across 603 modes. This is WORK: the missing mode is a shared-scope census that intersects providers with consumers AND respects import filters and definition nesting. UNWITNESSED because no command today distinguishes 'the mode is absent' from 'the mode is present under a spelling I did not try' -- it closes when such a mode exists.}
}

@misc{agda-F14-toolmodes-census-moved-mid-dispatch,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The toolmodes mode census can change mid-dispatch, so a within-session disagreement between two identical --find calls is contention, not a query bug.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=agda-oracle. Dispatched to find a decider for stranded RESIDUE.md binders. 'toolmodes.py --find residue' printed '(none)' under 'by MODE NAME containing residue' with footer '61 hit(s), 604 declared mode(s)'. Four calls later the byte-identical command printed '--stranded-residue on scratch/agda_defs.py' with footer '63 hit(s), 605 declared mode(s)'. I had begun composing a case-sensitivity finding to explain the difference. But '--find Residue' and '--find RESIDUE' both return 63, so --find is case-insensitive and that explanation was FALSE. The real cause is that the census is a swarm-contended artifact, the same shape as gate-F33-mode-census-is-a-swarm-contended-artifact, and agda_defs.stranded_residue was added under me. LESSON: an oracle re-deriving rather than recalling can still be wrong about WHY two of its own measurements differ, and the plausible explanation was reachable without checking. Re-run the identical query before theorising a mechanism for a within-dispatch disagreement. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): toolmodes --find residue returned '(none)' under 'by MODE NAME' with 61 hits, and the byte-identical command minutes later returned --stranded-residue with 63 hits. The mode census is a SWARM-CONTENDED artifact (cf gate-F33) and moved under a single dispatch. I nearly filed a case-sensitivity defect to explain the difference; --find is case-insensitive and there was no defect.},
  unification = {asserted}
}

