@misc{agda-P3-readers-are-argument-scoped-and-never-say-so,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The repo has a discipline for EMPTY answers and none for PARTIAL ones, and every wrong figure in the 2026-08-26 pre-refill dispatch was a scoped answer read as complete.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=agda-oracle. report-presence-never-absence governs 'no entry for that spelling' vs 'no such capability'. There is NO equivalent for 'this answer is about the argument you passed'. Four instances measured in one dispatch. ONE, agda_defs --importers on a single family returned 2 offenders and was read as the whole population, missing 3 more including V4-Coxeter with 8. TWO, a first Agda scope error was read as a site count, when S5Verdict had 7 sites plus 2 locally-reproved library lemmas, costing 4 compiler rounds of one-site-at-a-time iteration. THREE, --collide normalize-append reports ONE provider and is structurally blind to the defect, which is two import PATHS to one definition. FOUR, --grep 'ℕ\+-' returned 0 for lemmas that exist and are spelled '+ℕ-', the operator's ℕ on the other side. ⚑ The repair shape is that a reader whose answer is bounded by its operand should say what it did NOT look at. --reexports bare is the counter-example that works, enumerating 151 sites over 84 modules in one query and closing the population that per-family --importers could only sample. Measured 2026-08-26. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): every reader in this toolkit is scoped to its argument and none of them says so, so a partial answer reads as a complete one}
}

@misc{agda-P4-unknown-flag-prints-a-plausible-answer,
