  note     = {kind=finding by=agda-oracle via substrate. Witness registered in findings.py. DivStr in Wedge.agda has two law-free fields, z and recon, so a zero-free carrier such as the positive rationals may pick z=1 and it TYPECHECKS — coherent at that layer, since Trace's done fires at z. But Wedge/Mul.agda reads z (base M) as the MULTIPLICATIVE ZERO in its Nilpotent definition, and its own comment says so. ⚑ So the bare DivStr layer is safe for a zero-free carrier and the Mul enrichment is NOT — one field carrying two meanings across a layer boundary, with no law to catch the disagreement. ⚑⚑ GradedDivStr is the escape and this witness WATCHES it: one field, no laws, and no z at all, with the grade carrying termination instead of a distinguished element. If a z ever appears there the escape has closed, which is what makes this a live watch rather than a record. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): DivStr (Wedge.agda) has TWO law-free fields, `z : C` and `recon`, so a zero-free carrier such as ℚ>0 may pick z=1 and it TYPECHECKS — coherent, since Trace's `done` fires at z. But Wedge/Mul.agda reads `z (base M)` as the MULTIPLICATIVE ZERO (Nilpotent M x = Σ n → pow M x n ≡ z (base M); its comment: 'z, the wedge's terminal, is the multiplicative 0'). So z=1 on ℚ>0 yields a valid DivStr whose nilpotency notions are nonsense one layer up: the bare DivStr/Wedge layer is safe for a zero-free carrier, the Mul enrichment is NOT. ⚑ GradedDivStr is the escape and this witness watches it: ONE field, no laws, and NO `z` at all — the ℕ grade carries termination instead of a distinguished element, so ℚ>0 instantiates it with nothing to discharge. If a `z` ever appears there, that escape has closed}
}

